br.br:
Claim 1 (currently amended): An aircraft capable of vertical takeoff and landing, stationary flight and forward flight, the aircraft comprising:a first wing segment and a second wing segment;

a fuselage having a rear section and a nose section, the fuselage at least partially disposed between the first wing segment and the second wing segment;

three or more [[of ]]spokes coupling the fuselage to the first wing segment and the second wing segment;

three or more motors disposed within or attached to the plurality of three or more spokes in a distributed configuration; and

a propeller operably connected to each of the three or more motors;

wherein the nose section of the fuselage extends above the propellers, a leading edge of the first wing segment and a leading edge of the second wing segment when the aircraft is in vertical takeoff and landing, and

wherein the nose section of the fuselage extends forward beyond the propellers, the leading edge of the first wing segment and the leading edge of the second wing segment when the aircraft is in forward flight.



Claim 2 (original): The aircraft of claim 1, wherein the fuselage is configured to carry a payload.



Claim 3 (currently amended): The aircraft of claim 1, wherein the first and second wing segments, the fuselage and the plurality of three or more spokes are not substantially reconfigured for transition between vertical takeoff and landing, stationary flight and forward flight.









Claim 4 (original): The aircraft of claim 1, wherein the fuselage comprises two or more modules.



Claim 5 (original): The aircraft of claim 1, wherein the aircraft is unmanned.



Claim 6 (original): The aircraft of claim 1, wherein the each of the three or more propellers comprise a variable speed constant pitch propeller.



Claim 7 (currently amended): The aircraft of claim 1, wherein the plurality of three or more spokes are operable to change a position of the first or second wing segments with respect to the fuselage or vice versa.



Claim 8 (original): An aircraft capable of vertical takeoff and landing, stationary flight and forward flight, the aircraft comprising:a first wing segment and a second wing segment;

a fuselage having a rear section and a nose section, the fuselage at least partially disposed between the first wing segment and the second wing segment;

a plurality of spokes coupling the fuselage to the first wing segment and the second wing segment;

one or more motors disposed within or attached to the plurality of spokes;

a propeller proximate to a leading edge of each of the plurality of spokes and operably connected to the one or more motors;

wherein the nose section of the fuselage extends above the propellers, a leading edge of the first wing segment and a leading edge of the second wing segment when the aircraft is in vertical takeoff and landing, and

wherein the nose section of the fuselage extends forward beyond the propellers, the leading edge of the first wing segment and the leading edge of the second wing segment when the aircraft is in forward flight.



Claim 9 (original): The aircraft of claim 8, wherein the fuselage is configured to carry a payload.









Claim 10 (original): The aircraft of claim 8, wherein the first and second wing segments, the fuselage and the plurality of spokes are not substantially reconfigured for transition between vertical takeoff and landing, stationary flight and forward flight.



Claim 11 (original): The aircraft of claim 8, wherein the fuselage comprises two or more modules.



Claim 12 (original): The aircraft of claim 8, wherein the aircraft is unmanned.



Claim 13 (original): The aircraft of claim 8, wherein the propeller is a variable speed constant pitch propeller.



Claim 14 (currently amended): An aircraft capable of vertical takeoff and landing, stationary flight and forward flight, the aircraft comprising:a fuselage having a rear section and a nose section, the fuselage at least partially disposed between [[the]]a first wing segment and [[the]]a second wing segment;

six spokes coupled to the fuselage, wherein at least two of the spokes are coupled to the first wing segment and at least two of the spokes are coupled to the second wing segment;

one or more motors disposed within or attached to the six spokes; and

a propeller proximate to a leading edge of each of the six spokes, and operably connected to the one or more motors;

wherein the nose section of the fuselage extends above the propellers, a leading edge of the first wing segment and a leading edge of the second wing segment when the aircraft is in vertical takeoff and landing, and

wherein the nose section of the fuselage extends forward beyond the propellers, the leading edge of the first wing segment and the leading edge of the second wing segment when the aircraft is in forward flight.



Claim 15 (original): The aircraft of claim 14, wherein the fuselage is configured to carry a payload.









Claim 16 (currently amended): The aircraft of claim 14, wherein the first and second wing segments, the fuselage and the plurality of sixspokes are not substantially reconfigured for transition between vertical takeoff and landing, stationary flight and forward flight.



Claim 17 (original): The aircraft of claim 14, wherein the fuselage comprises two or more modules.



Claim 18 (original): The aircraft of claim 14, wherein the aircraft is unmanned.



Claim 19 (original): The aircraft of claim 14, wherein the propeller is a variable speed constant pitch propeller.



Claim 20 (cancelled)



Claim 21 (new): The aircraft of claim 14, wherein the six spokes are operable to change a position of the first or second wing segments with respect to the fuselage or vice versa.
